# 061-people-links.lua

Keeps `rao-home/people/`: one folder per person, holding one link per top
room you share with them, so `ls people/<person>` answers "what do I share
with this person?".  The rooms' recipient lists are the truth; the links are
a view rebuilt from them, so deleting `people/` and rebuilding gives the same
result.  Loaded with `require("061-people-links")` after the load path
(`src/room-folders/001-load-path.lua`).  Needs `002-room-folders` (issue 101)
at load; reads 103's `rooms/<id>/split` marker file directly.  Does not need
102: "top of a tree" is found by one step up (a room the person receives
whose parent they do not), which gives the same answer as walking 102's
ancestors (checked by `tests/072-test-with-neighbours.lua`).

Types: **home** is `{dir = string}`; a **room id** is a string of 32
lower-case hex characters; a **contact** is a string, a name from the
contacts file.

## Outward functions

| function | takes | gives | purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `top_rooms_for(home, contact)` | home, string | list of string (room ids, sorted) | The top room of each tree this person is in, as you see it.  For an own device: nothing under `people.own-devices = none`; every tree's top under `all-tops`. |
| `planned_links(home, contact)` | home, string | list of `{path = string, target = string, room_id = string, folder = string, name = string}`, sorted by `path` | What `people/<contact>/` should hold, without touching the disk.  `path` is inside the person's folder (`"home"` or `"<room id>/home"`); `target` is the relative link text (`"../../rooms/<id>"` or `"../../../rooms/<id>"`); `folder` is `""` or the room id. |
| `rebuild_person(home, contact)` | home, string | nothing | Makes `people/<contact>/` hold exactly the planned links: removes this module's stale links, keeps anything else, creates missing links, removes the folder if it ends up empty.  Errors if something that is not its own link sits where a link must go, or if the name cannot be a folder name. |
| `rebuild_all(home)` | home | nothing | Rebuilds everyone on any list, every own device under `all-tops`, and every folder already in `people/` (so people on no list any more are swept). |
| `read_snapshot(home)` | home | table (see below) | One read of every room, contact and choice, shared by a whole rebuild.  Outward only for tests and the demo. |

A snapshot is `{modes = {collision, own_devices, split_records} (strings),
room_ids = list of string, rooms = room id -> {id, name, parent_id, recipients
= list of string}, visible = room id -> bool, contacts = name -> {field ->
string}, recipients = sorted list of string, slash_mode = string}`.

## Config keys read

| key | when | values |
|---|---|---|
| `people.link-collision` | every rebuild | `suffix-id`, `numbered`, `id-folder` (see 063) |
| `people.own-devices` | every rebuild | `none`, `all-tops` |
| `people.split-records` | every rebuild | `show`, `hide` (a room with 103's split marker is skipped under `hide`) |
| `rooms.unknown-parent` (101's) | only when a parent id names no room here | `keep`: that room is a top; `refuse`: error |
| `rooms.slash-in-name` (101's) | only when a room name holds `/` | `allow` needed, anything else errors |
| `people.slash-in-link-name` | only when a room name holds `/` | `percent` (`%2F`), `division-slash` (U+2215) |

All are read with `config_value`: a missing key is an error, never a default.

## What it listens for

Registers on 101's change bell at load: `"created"`, `"name"`, `"parent"`,
`"recipients"`, `"removed-room"`, `"split"` → `rebuild_all`; `"log"` → nothing;
any other kind → error.

## What it errors on

A recipient who is not a contact, or who is an own device; a contact name
that cannot be a folder name; `people/` being a link; anything that is not
this module's link sitting where a link must go.

## What it never touches

Anything in `people/` that is not a link of exactly the shapes
`people/<c>/<name> -> ../../rooms/<32 hex>` or
`people/<c>/<32 hex>/<name> -> ../../../rooms/<32 hex>`.  A person's folder
that is itself a link is never entered.
